Primitive Cell Abstraction & Type Hiding

typed_cells_demo.cpp

1. Objective & Technical Scope
The primary goal of this module was to construct and demonstrate the core primitive data container; the Value class. The Value class serves as a safe, encapsulated scalar cell capable of holding diverse C++ primitive types while maintaining strict data integrity and type abstraction.

2. My Implementation Process
To implement this part, I focused on the following steps:

Instantiating Multi-Type Data Cells:

Integer Representation: I declared Value int_cell(42); to store discrete integer values (e.g., counts, IDs, discrete metrics).

Floating-Point Representation: I declared Value double_cell(3.14); to encapsulate continuous floating-point numbers (e.g., ratios, measurements, scaled scores).

String Representation: I declared Value string_cell(std::string("Makerere")); to store textual metadata and labels.

Encapsulation & Type Hiding Mechanics:

Inside the Value class, the underlying storage mechanism (utilizing a std::variant<int, double, std::string>) is kept strictly private.

By keeping internal members private, I ensured that external code cannot corrupt the internal state or perform unsafe direct pointer/type manipulation.

Access to stored data is controlled exclusively through explicit conversion helpers (such as as_number()) and explicit getters like getType().

Runtime Safety & Exception Control:

I wrapped the initialization block inside a C++ try-catch structure.

By catching const std::exception& e references at the top level, I guaranteed that any standard library exception or invalid conversion attempt is trapped gracefully and logged via std::cerr rather than causing an unhandled program collapse.

3. Verification & Results
Upon compiling and executing typed_cells_demo.cpp, all three Value instances were instantiated successfully without type collisions or memory leaks, confirming that heterogeneous data points can be handled safely within a single cohesive wrapper class.
